# Molt - thirty-seven patches and an Init, voiced against the debug build.
#
# The last machine in the app to get a bank, and the one where "a bank" needs
# explaining. Molt has no sound of its own. It takes a take somebody sang,
# finds the glottal pulses in it, and lays them down again at a spacing the
# clip decides - so pitch and formant move independently and the piano roll
# does the tuning. What a patch here stores is therefore a **way of treating
# a voice you supply**, the same shape of thing Dice's eighteen store for a
# loop. Mount a different take and every patch still means what it says.
#
# That is why there are thirty-seven and not sixty. Sweeping `formant` across
# its range would be twelve near-duplicates of one voice; what earns a patch
# is doing something nameable.
#
# **The two controls the machine is really about.** `tune` is how hard the
# sung line is pulled onto what was written - at nought the line survives and
# is merely moved to the note, at one every moment lands exactly on it.
# `rate` is how long that takes. The pair is the whole classic vocabulary:
# 1/0 is the hard tune everybody knows, 0.75/60 is a singer correcting
# themselves, 1/250 scoops into every note.
#
# **`rate` has nothing to do at tune 1 and that is correct**, not a fault. At
# full tune the target is the written note and it does not move, so there is
# nothing to glide towards; the smoothing only shows where the target moves,
# which is at partial tune or under a bend. Rendering tune 1 at rate 0 and at
# rate 400 gives bit-identical audio, and a quarter of an hour was spent
# proving that is the machine being right.
#
# **Partial tune measures as out of tune, and should.** The harness anchors on
# the note it asked for, so a patch sitting deliberately between the sung
# pitch and the written one reads a hundred cents flat with almost no energy
# on the note's harmonic series. `Correct` and `Natural` below are the two
# that do it; their tune and harm columns are the feature, not a fault.
#
# `Pluck` measures four decibels under the rest and `volume` is already at the
# top of its range, so it stays there. The reason is in its own note: a sound
# that has mostly gone inside a tenth of a second spends most of the window
# the bank is levelled in silent, and levelling it up by the number would put
# its peak above everything else in the bank.
#
# **Voiced against a real recording.** The harness mounts the file named in
# tools/local.env when there is one and a twelve second synthetic phrase when
# there is not, and this bank is levelled on the former - a phone in a room,
# which is what this machine will always be fed. The numbers in the table
# below are that take's; on the synthetic phrase the same volumes come out
# 1.5 dB quieter and 1.5 dB closer together, which is as near as two different
# voices can be expected to land.
#
# What the earlier version of this bank was levelled against, and why its
# numbers were wrong: the synthetic phrase has no rumble, no clipping, a crest
# factor of eleven decibels and audio from its first frame. The recording is
# fifty-six per cent sub-seventy-hertz energy, clipped at 0.3% of its samples,
# thirty-three decibels of crest, and four hundred milliseconds of room tone
# before the first word - and every note began in that room. The harness
# measures a tenth of a second in, so `harm`, `part` and `low` were all being
# read off silence. `Utterance::analyse` takes the rumble and the room out
# now; see engine/core/Utterance.h.
#
# The fallback phrase is twelve seconds and its pitch walks between 70 and
# 190 Hz across nine syllables. It used to be a two and a half second one at
# a fixed pitch, against which `tune` at nought and `tune` at one differ by a
# constant offset and `rate` has nothing at all to act on - the same mistake
# as judging a vocoder on a held vowel.
#
# Stepped parameters are written as `#n`, and `#n` is the *step index*, not
# the value:
#   robot      #0 off  #1 every mark takes the written pitch
#   loop       #0 the take plays once  #1 it runs round
#   filtertype #0 LP6 #1 LP12 #2 LP18 #3 LP24  #4 HP6 #5 HP12 #6 HP18 #7 HP24
#              #8 BP6 #9 BP12 #10 notch #11 peak
#   octave     -3..3, so #3 is nought and #2 is an octave down
#   transpose  -12..12, so #12 is nought
#   bendrange  0..24 semitones, so #12 is an octave
#
# Families: tune, robot, size, tone, shape, play.
#
# Every patch but Init carries eight named macros. Destinations are numbered
# as in Molt.h's MacroDest: #1 start, #2 tune, #3 rate, #4 formant, #5 mega,
# #6 cutoff, #7 resonance, #8 attack, #9 decay, #10 sustain, #11 release,
# #12 velocity, #13 drive, #14 volume, #15 pan, #16 pitch (24 semitones a
# unit). Each family has its own set of eight. A macro whose knob has no room
# left goes the other way under the opposite name (Smaller, Brighter, Clean,
# Octave Up...). Where a macro at full moves the level by more than a
# decibel, a volume route takes it back out; where the volume is already at
# the top, the macro moves less instead. Talkbox and Telephone have Slow In
# where the others have Later: moving the start lands their narrow band on
# a different vowel and the level jumps with it.

machine Molt

patch Init family=tune

patch Natural family=tune
  # The line as it was sung, moved to the note and otherwise left alone. The
  # one to hear the take itself on, and what every other patch here is a
  # departure from. It reads badly out of tune on purpose: that is the
  # singer's own line showing through.
  tune           0.0
  rate           40 ms
  volume         0.75
  set macro1 "Pull"
  mac1_d1  #2
  mac1_a1  0.55
  mac1_d2  #14
  mac1_a2  0.108
  set macro2 "Slower"
  mac2_d1  #3
  mac2_a1  0.3
  set macro3 "Bigger"
  mac3_d1  #4
  mac3_a1  -0.2
  mac3_d2  #14
  mac3_a2  0.182
  set macro4 "Darker"
  mac4_d1  #6
  mac4_a1  -0.25
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.219
  set macro6 "Horn"
  mac6_d1  #5
  mac6_a1  0.6
  set macro7 "Later"
  mac7_d1  #1
  mac7_a1  0.3
  mac7_d2  #14
  mac7_a2  0.167
  set macro8 "Octave Down"
  mac8_d1  #16
  mac8_a1  -0.5
  mac8_d2  #14
  mac8_a2  0.101

patch Nudge family=tune
  # A little way onto the note, slowly, so the sung line keeps its shape and
  # only the notes furthest out are pulled in. Between Natural and Correct.
  tune           0.45
  rate           120 ms
  volume         0.89
  set macro1 "Pull"
  mac1_d1  #2
  mac1_a1  0.55
  set macro2 "Slower"
  mac2_d1  #3
  mac2_a1  0.3
  set macro3 "Bigger"
  mac3_d1  #4
  mac3_a1  -0.2
  set macro4 "Darker"
  mac4_d1  #6
  mac4_a1  -0.25
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.286
  set macro6 "Horn"
  mac6_d1  #5
  mac6_a1  0.6
  mac6_d2  #14
  mac6_a2  -0.188
  set macro7 "Later"
  mac7_d1  #1
  mac7_a1  0.3
  mac7_d2  #14
  mac7_a2  0.198
  set macro8 "Octave Down"
  mac8_d1  #16
  mac8_a1  -0.5
  mac8_d2  #14
  mac8_a2  -0.082

patch Correct family=tune
  # A singer correcting themselves: most of the way onto the note, over about
  # the time a person takes to notice. The setting to use when you want the
  # performance and not the effect.
  tune           0.78
  rate           60 ms
  volume         0.9
  set macro1 "Looser"
  mac1_d1  #2
  mac1_a1  -0.5
  mac1_d2  #14
  mac1_a2  -0.083
  set macro2 "Slower"
  mac2_d1  #3
  mac2_a1  0.3
  set macro3 "Bigger"
  mac3_d1  #4
  mac3_a1  -0.2
  set macro4 "Darker"
  mac4_d1  #6
  mac4_a1  -0.25
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.296
  set macro6 "Horn"
  mac6_d1  #5
  mac6_a1  0.6
  mac6_d2  #14
  mac6_a2  -0.199
  set macro7 "Later"
  mac7_d1  #1
  mac7_a1  0.3
  mac7_d2  #14
  mac7_a2  0.267
  set macro8 "Octave Down"
  mac8_d1  #16
  mac8_a1  -0.5
  mac8_d2  #14
  mac8_a2  -0.095

patch Hard family=tune
  # The one everybody knows. Every moment on the note, arrived at instantly,
  # so the line moves in steps rather than glides and the steps are the sound.
  #
  # Tune and rate here are the machine's own defaults, so this patch would be
  # Init with a different name if it stopped there - it carries the bite the
  # effect has in practice, which is where the difference actually is.
  tune           1.0
  rate           0 ms
  drive          0.3
  cutoff         7000 Hz
  filtertype     #1
  volume         0.59
  set macro1 "Looser"
  mac1_d1  #2
  mac1_a1  -0.5
  set macro2 "Slower"
  mac2_d1  #3
  mac2_a1  0.3
  set macro3 "Bigger"
  mac3_d1  #4
  mac3_a1  -0.2
  mac3_d2  #14
  mac3_a2  -0.035
  set macro4 "Darker"
  mac4_d1  #6
  mac4_a1  -0.25
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.062
  set macro6 "Horn"
  mac6_d1  #5
  mac6_a1  0.6
  mac6_d2  #14
  mac6_a2  -0.105
  set macro7 "Later"
  mac7_d1  #1
  mac7_a1  0.3
  mac7_d2  #14
  mac7_a2  0.119
  set macro8 "Octave Down"
  mac8_d1  #16
  mac8_a1  -0.5
  mac8_d2  #14
  mac8_a2  -0.043

patch "Catch Up" family=tune
  # Full correction arriving late, so every note is scooped into from
  # wherever the voice was. The other half of the classic pair, and the one
  # that sounds like singing rather than like arithmetic.
  #
  # Tune short of one on purpose: at exactly one the target is the written
  # note and never moves, so there is nothing for a slow rate to chase and
  # this would render bit-identical to `Hard`. A hair of the sung line left
  # in is what the scoop is made of.
  tune           0.95
  rate           250 ms
  volume         0.93
  set macro1 "Looser"
  mac1_d1  #2
  mac1_a1  -0.5
  set macro2 "Quicker"
  mac2_d1  #3
  mac2_a1  -0.5
  set macro3 "Bigger"
  mac3_d1  #4
  mac3_a1  -0.2
  set macro4 "Darker"
  mac4_d1  #6
  mac4_a1  -0.25
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.309
  set macro6 "Horn"
  mac6_d1  #5
  mac6_a1  0.6
  mac6_d2  #14
  mac6_a2  -0.176
  set macro7 "Later"
  mac7_d1  #1
  mac7_a1  0.3
  mac7_d2  #14
  mac7_a2  0.236
  set macro8 "Octave Down"
  mac8_d1  #16
  mac8_a1  -0.5
  mac8_d2  #14
  mac8_a2  -0.110

patch Drag family=tune
  # Every note scooped into from a long way off, slower than Catch Up, so a
  # quick line never quite lands and a held note slides home.
  tune           0.97
  rate           400 ms
  volume         0.93
  set macro1 "Looser"
  mac1_d1  #2
  mac1_a1  -0.5
  set macro2 "Quicker"
  mac2_d1  #3
  mac2_a1  -0.5
  set macro3 "Bigger"
  mac3_d1  #4
  mac3_a1  -0.2
  set macro4 "Darker"
  mac4_d1  #6
  mac4_a1  -0.25
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.302
  set macro6 "Horn"
  mac6_d1  #5
  mac6_a1  0.6
  mac6_d2  #14
  mac6_a2  -0.171
  set macro7 "Later"
  mac7_d1  #1
  mac7_a1  0.3
  mac7_d2  #14
  mac7_a2  0.256
  set macro8 "Octave Down"
  mac8_d1  #16
  mac8_a1  -0.5
  mac8_d2  #14
  mac8_a2  -0.092

patch Robot family=robot
  # Every mark takes the written pitch, voiced or not, so the consonants come
  # with it and the words survive a monotone. This is the machine's other
  # headline and it needs no filter to be itself.
  robot          #1
  volume         0.91
  set macro1 "Bigger"
  mac1_d1  #4
  mac1_a1  -0.2
  mac1_d2  #14
  mac1_a2  -0.053
  set macro2 "Horn"
  mac2_d1  #5
  mac2_a1  0.6
  mac2_d2  #14
  mac2_a2  -0.187
  set macro3 "Darker"
  mac3_d1  #6
  mac3_a1  -0.25
  set macro4 "Resonant"
  mac4_d1  #7
  mac4_a1  0.5
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.303
  set macro6 "Longer"
  mac6_d1  #11
  mac6_a1  0.3
  set macro7 "Later"
  mac7_d1  #1
  mac7_a1  0.3
  mac7_d2  #14
  mac7_a2  0.221
  set macro8 "Octave Down"
  mac8_d1  #16
  mac8_a1  -0.5
  mac8_d2  #14
  mac8_a2  -0.096

patch Choir family=robot
  # The signature. One read head, four voices: draw a chord and the take
  # sings it, all four saying the same word at the same moment and differing
  # only in what they are pulled to. Glide so the chord moves rather than
  # jumps.
  robot          #1
  glide          0.12 s
  amprelease     0.4 s
  volume         0.91
  set macro1 "Bigger"
  mac1_d1  #4
  mac1_a1  -0.2
  mac1_d2  #14
  mac1_a2  -0.053
  set macro2 "Horn"
  mac2_d1  #5
  mac2_a1  0.6
  mac2_d2  #14
  mac2_a2  -0.187
  set macro3 "Darker"
  mac3_d1  #6
  mac3_a1  -0.25
  set macro4 "Resonant"
  mac4_d1  #7
  mac4_a1  0.5
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.303
  set macro6 "Longer"
  mac6_d1  #11
  mac6_a1  0.3
  set macro7 "Later"
  mac7_d1  #1
  mac7_a1  0.3
  mac7_d2  #14
  mac7_a2  0.221
  set macro8 "Octave Down"
  mac8_d1  #16
  mac8_a1  -0.5
  mac8_d2  #14
  mac8_a2  -0.096

patch "Tin Voice" family=robot
  # The monotone through a little of the horn and a slightly smaller throat,
  # so the voice sounds like it comes out of a toy.
  robot          #1
  mega           0.35
  formant        +2 st
  cutoff         5000 Hz
  filtertype     #1
  drive          0.2
  volume         0.47
  set macro1 "Bigger"
  mac1_d1  #4
  mac1_a1  -0.2
  mac1_d2  #14
  mac1_a2  0.081
  set macro2 "Horn"
  mac2_d1  #5
  mac2_a1  0.6
  mac2_d2  #14
  mac2_a2  -0.084
  set macro3 "Darker"
  mac3_d1  #6
  mac3_a1  -0.25
  mac3_d2  #14
  mac3_a2  0.298
  set macro4 "Resonant"
  mac4_d1  #7
  mac4_a1  0.5
  mac4_d2  #14
  mac4_a2  -0.034
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.070
  set macro6 "Longer"
  mac6_d1  #11
  mac6_a1  0.3
  set macro7 "Later"
  mac7_d1  #1
  mac7_a1  0.3
  mac7_d2  #14
  mac7_a2  0.237
  set macro8 "Octave Down"
  mac8_d1  #16
  mac8_a1  -0.5

patch Talkbox family=robot
  # A monotone through a resonant band, which is the cheap way to the sound a
  # tube in the mouth makes. The resonance is doing the vowel work here, not
  # the take.
  robot          #1
  filtertype     #8
  cutoff         1200 Hz
  resonance      0.62
  volume         1.5
  set macro1 "Bigger"
  mac1_d1  #4
  mac1_a1  -0.08
  set macro2 "Horn"
  mac2_d1  #5
  mac2_a1  0.6
  mac2_d2  #14
  mac2_a2  -0.516
  set macro3 "Brighter"
  mac3_d1  #6
  mac3_a1  0.087
  set macro4 "Smooth"
  mac4_d1  #7
  mac4_a1  -0.4
  mac4_d2  #14
  mac4_a2  -0.159
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.538
  set macro6 "Longer"
  mac6_d1  #11
  mac6_a1  0.3
  set macro7 "Slow In"
  mac7_d1  #8
  mac7_a1  0.35
  set macro8 "Octave Down"
  mac8_d1  #16
  mac8_a1  -0.5
  mac8_d2  #14
  mac8_a2  -0.178

patch Monk family=robot
  # The written pitch an octave down, slow to leave. A monotone low enough
  # that the formants rather than the pitch carry the word.
  robot          #1
  octave         #2
  formant        -3 st
  amprelease     0.9 s
  volume         0.9
  set macro1 "Bigger"
  mac1_d1  #4
  mac1_a1  -0.2
  mac1_d2  #14
  mac1_a2  0.138
  set macro2 "Horn"
  mac2_d1  #5
  mac2_a1  0.6
  set macro3 "Darker"
  mac3_d1  #6
  mac3_a1  -0.25
  set macro4 "Resonant"
  mac4_d1  #7
  mac4_a1  0.5
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.263
  set macro6 "Longer"
  mac6_d1  #11
  mac6_a1  0.3
  set macro7 "Later"
  mac7_d1  #1
  mac7_a1  0.3
  mac7_d2  #14
  mac7_a2  0.200
  set macro8 "Octave Up"
  mac8_d1  #16
  mac8_a1  0.5
  mac8_d2  #14
  mac8_a2  0.182

patch Mantra family=robot
  # A monotone that runs round the take for as long as the note is held,
  # coming in slowly and leaving slowly. Words as a drone.
  robot          #1
  loop           #1
  formant        -2 st
  cutoff         3000 Hz
  filtertype     #1
  ampattack      0.25 s
  amprelease     1.8 s
  volume         0.95
  set macro1 "Bigger"
  mac1_d1  #4
  mac1_a1  -0.2
  set macro2 "Horn"
  mac2_d1  #5
  mac2_a1  0.6
  set macro3 "Darker"
  mac3_d1  #6
  mac3_a1  -0.25
  mac3_d2  #14
  mac3_a2  0.111
  set macro4 "Resonant"
  mac4_d1  #7
  mac4_a1  0.5
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.305
  set macro6 "Shorter"
  mac6_d1  #11
  mac6_a1  -0.3
  set macro7 "Later"
  mac7_d1  #1
  mac7_a1  0.3
  mac7_d2  #14
  mac7_a2  0.251
  set macro8 "Octave Down"
  mac8_d1  #16
  mac8_a1  -0.5
  mac8_d2  #14
  mac8_a2  -0.094

patch "Grown Up" family=size
  # A throat a little larger than the singer's own. The plainest of the size
  # patches: the same voice, older.
  formant        -2 st
  volume         0.89
  set macro1 "Bigger"
  mac1_d1  #4
  mac1_a1  -0.2
  mac1_d2  #14
  mac1_a2  0.057
  set macro2 "Darker"
  mac2_d1  #6
  mac2_a1  -0.25
  set macro3 "Horn"
  mac3_d1  #5
  mac3_a1  0.6
  mac3_d2  #14
  mac3_a2  -0.088
  set macro4 "Resonant"
  mac4_d1  #7
  mac4_a1  0.5
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.286
  set macro6 "Looser"
  mac6_d1  #2
  mac6_a1  -0.5
  set macro7 "Later"
  mac7_d1  #1
  mac7_a1  0.3
  mac7_d2  #14
  mac7_a2  0.189
  set macro8 "Octave Down"
  mac8_d1  #16
  mac8_a1  -0.5
  mac8_d2  #14
  mac8_a2  -0.094

patch Giant family=size
  # The same note sung by somebody twice the size. Nothing here touches the
  # pitch at all - this is the resonances of the throat moving on their own,
  # which is the thing a sampler cannot do.
  formant        -8 st
  volume         1.22
  set macro1 "Smaller"
  mac1_d1  #4
  mac1_a1  0.2
  mac1_d2  #14
  mac1_a2  -0.210
  set macro2 "Darker"
  mac2_d1  #6
  mac2_a1  -0.25
  set macro3 "Horn"
  mac3_d1  #5
  mac3_a1  0.6
  mac3_d2  #14
  mac3_a2  -0.167
  set macro4 "Resonant"
  mac4_d1  #7
  mac4_a1  0.5
  set macro5 "Grit"
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac5_d2  #14
  mac5_a2  -0.420
  set macro6 "Looser"
  mac6_d1  #2
  mac6_a1  -0.5
  mac6_d2  #14
  mac6_a2  -0.217
  set macro7 "Later"
  mac7_d1  #1
  mac7_a1  0.18
  mac7_d2  #14
